WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.214
One glass can provide different structural cues in RGB and thermal.

2
00:00:03.214 --> 00:00:06.964
Visible light from the background enters and passes through the glass.

3
00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:11.786
The RGB camera can receive background information through the glass walls.

4
00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:13.929
In long-wave thermal imaging, the glass has a different structural response.

5
00:00:13.929 --> 00:00:20.357
The thermal camera mainly receives cues from the glass surface.

6
00:00:20.357 --> 00:00:25.714
Background cues and glass-surface cues describe different physical depths.

7
00:00:25.714 --> 00:00:30.000
These conflicting geometric cues make RGB–thermal alignment ambiguous.

8
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
RGB and thermal images provide two separate reconstruction inputs.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
RGB reconstruction recovers cup edges and the background seen through the glass.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Thermal reconstruction recovers the glass surface as orange points.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The blue RGB cloud contains background geometry behind the cup.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
RGB background information is transferred into the thermal estimate.

13
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Some orange surface points gradually turn red and move away from the cup surface.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
The affected points drift toward background depth, producing incorrect thermal geometry.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
RGB background geometry and thermal surface geometry describe different structures.

16
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
This interference motivates task-relevant RGB–thermal alignment.
